            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein keto friendly?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey isolate is. It is filtered to remove most of the lactose, leaving about 0 to 2 g of carbohydrate per scoop in most flavors. Whey concentrate carries more lactose, often 3 to 5 g of carbohydrate per scoop, which still fits many keto budgets but needs counting. Check the panel for your flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many carbs should a keto protein powder have?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no official threshold, but a good keto protein powder has about 3 g of total carbohydrate or less per serving. That keeps one shake to a small share of a typical 20 to 50 g daily keto limit. Prefer products that reach that number without subtracting sugar alcohols or large amounts of fiber.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Isopure Zero Carb really have zero carbs?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per the label, yes: Isopure Zero Carb lists 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop. US labeling rules allow less than 0.5 g per serving to be declared as zero, so a scoop may contain a trace. That is negligible even on strict keto. It is sweetened with sucralose rather than sugar alcohols.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Will protein powder kick me out of ketosis?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A low-carb powder is very unlikely to. The carbohydrate in an isolate is tiny, and gluconeogenesis, where the body makes glucose from protein, is driven by demand rather than by eating moderate protein. Most people stay in ketosis with typical protein intakes. If you test ketones and see a change, adjust portion sizes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are sugar alcohols bad on keto?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on which one. Erythritol barely affects blood sugar, though a 2023 Nature Medicine study linked high blood levels to cardiovascular events, which is unproven but worth noting. Maltitol raises blood sugar and commonly causes digestive upset. Many of the best low-carb powders avoid sugar alcohols entirely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there a low carb vegan protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, if you buy unflavored. Single-source pea or soy isolates can carry about 2 g of carbohydrate per serving, as unflavored Naked Pea does per the brand. Flavored plant blends often carry 5 to 10 g. Flavor an unflavored isolate yourself with cocoa, cinnamon or coffee to keep the carbs down.
                </p>
              </div>
